  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log (WAL) is a data structure that stores a history of all operations performed on the database. It If a database were to crash and all of its data were to be lost, explain in detail how the write-ahead log would help the database recover.\n\n## Step 1: Understanding Write-Ahead Logging\nA write-ahead log is a technique used by databases to ensure data consistency and durability in the face of system failures. It works by requiring the database to log all modifications before they are actually written to the main data storage. This means every change made to the database's data is recorded in a log file before it is applied to the actual data storage.\n\n## Step 2: How Write-Ahead Log Helps Recovery\nWhen a database crashes, it is possible that modifications made to the data were not persisted to the main storage before the crash. This is where the write-ahead log comes in. By consulting the log file, the database can determine which operations were still pending at the time of the crash and how they should be applied to the data storage to make it consistent with the state of the log.\n\n## Step 3: Recovery Process\nTo recover from a crash, the database reads the log from the point where the last change was successfully written to the main storage before the crash. It then replays all the operations recorded in the log after that point, applying them to"
